On the patch:

> -	memcpy((u8 *)dst, (u8 *)src, size);
> +	/* The appended firmware may overlap its fixed execution address. */
> +	memmove((u8 *)dst, (u8 *)src, size);

Thanks for tracking this down and including concrete overlap numbers
for R2S (6680 bytes) and RV2 (7704 bytes) in the commit message -- that
makes the bug very easy to believe and reason about.

A few review notes based on the actual relocation logic in this hunk:

1. Direction of the fix is correct. Since dst = DDR_FIRMWARE_BASE is a
   fixed low address and src = pos grows upward as SPL/DT data appends
   the firmware image, dst < src always holds here, i.e. the
   destination always starts *before* the source. A forward byte/word
   copy (what glibc/most memcpy implementations use, including the
   SPL copy routine on RV) is actually safe for dst < src overlap --
   it's the dst > src case that corrupts data with a forward memcpy.
   Can you confirm empirically (as your validation notes claim) that
   dst < src is guaranteed never to flip, e.g. if a future board has
   DDR_FIRMWARE_BASE placed *after* the appended blob's start? If that
   invariant isn't architecturally guaranteed, memmove() is the right
   defensive choice regardless, but it would help to document *why*
   dst is always <= src (or just state memmove is used unconditionally
   for safety, independent of the direction argument).

2. Edge cases:
   - Zero-size regions: if size == 0 (e.g. firmware header reports no
     payload), memmove(dst, src, 0) is a no-op and safe, same as
     memcpy was. No new issue introduced here.
   - Exact overlap (src == dst, fully in place): memmove degrades to a
     no-op copy correctly; memcpy with src == dst is also harmless in
     practice but technically UB per the C standard. memmove formally
     covers this case, which is a nice side benefit even though it's
     not the motivating scenario.
   - Partial overlap at the tail end (the actual bug, dst+size > src
     with dst < src): memmove with a backwards-copying implementation
     (copy highest-to-lowest address first) handles this correctly.
     Worth double-checking that the memmove() pulled in for the SPL
     build (which libc/compiler builtins are available in this early
     boot context -- is this U-Boot's own lib/string.c memmove or a
     compiler builtin?) actually implements the standard semantics and
     isn't a trivial/stripped-down SPL variant that silently falls
     back to forward copy for "size" optimization reasons. That would
     defeat the whole fix.

3. Alignment: the existing code does `size = round_up(size, 64)` only
   *after* the copy, for cache-maintenance purposes it seems (based on
   the subsequent context about flushing). Since memmove/memcpy don't
   depend on alignment for correctness, this isn't a problem, but it's
   worth confirming the round_up'd region (used later for the icache
   invalidation, per the comment about "Ensure ... DDR firmware bytes
   are observable") doesn't also need the overlap-safe copy semantics
   applied to it -- i.e. that no other memcpy() call sites downstream
   in this same relocation path have the identical overlap risk and
   were simply not caught here.

4. No performance concern: memmove's extra overlap check only runs
   once per boot at this call site, so the mild overhead vs. memcpy is
   irrelevant for SPL DDR training microcode size.

No Reviewed-by or other review tags have been applied to this patch
yet.

Verdict: Looks correct for the described forward-copy overlap bug;
would like confirmation on point 1 (is dst always <= src by
construction, or is memmove purely defensive) and point 2 (which
memmove implementation is linked into this SPL build) before this is
ready to pick up.
